{
  "case_id": "2002-01-950",
  "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This is a case of inventive statutory interpretation by the Ninth Circuit. The statute at issue directs the courts how to construe this act or any other provision of law, yet the Ninth Circuit interpreted the statute rather inventively as an exemption from a provision of the Constitution."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: We'll hear argument next in Number 01-950, the Hillside Dairy, Inc. v. William J. Lyons, Jr., and a companion case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But as far as that clause is concerned it wouldn't help you with the corporate, if you had a corporate farmer. It would only work for individuals."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Barbara B. McDowell",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0000-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Section 144 does not indicate with unmistakable clarity that Congress meant to exempt from the Commerce Clause any California laws, much less to exempt the particular raw milk pooling regulations at issue here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: How do you respond to your adversary's contention that when you use the word construe, Congress does not ordinarily tell this Court how to construe a provision of the Constitution?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Urban",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First of all, in the McCarran-Ferguson Act statutes the Court found that there was a Dormant Commerce Clause exemption."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Urban",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0001-0002",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If you didn't have pricing and pooling laws and you went back to the free market, you'd be subject to the same boom-bust cycle that led to the creation of these pricing and pooling laws in the first place, and when you'd reached the point where the prices were very high and supplies were low, I think the -- the inevitable result of that would be that the composition standards would be undone and they'd simply revert to the Federal standards."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Urban",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That -- that's exactly right. The in-State producers are regulated. They get a guaranteed minimum price. Out-of --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: What do you do with the Chalker case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Urban",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "On the first issue we believe it's unmistakably clear that there is an exemption established by 7254 to the Dormant Commerce Clause."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Urban",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "As to the second issue, we believe that California's milk pooling and pricing laws are within the scope of section 7254."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Urban",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Urban pointed out that the McCarran-Ferguson Act uses the verb construe, and rightly so, but what it says the Court is not to construe as forbidding State regulation is Congress' silence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I do think -- Justice Stevens asked Ms. McDowell if the Court needs to reach the Privileges and Immunities Clause claim."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Englert",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0002-0003",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That's the essence of the Commerce Clause violation in this case, and it's also inconceivable that that kind of regulation could be protected by this statute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Urban",
      "case_id": "2002-01-950",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "93d1f755b6dd0664f13269436c26a584b97b6f1eb7007ba7a932b41e031d0f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-93d1f755b6dd-0002-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Urban said that 10 to 15 percent of the milk that comes into California is from out of State. I understand from a publication that's cited at page 39 of our opening brief called Dairy Profit Weekly that the actual number is closer to 3 percent."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
